A||||4||fiero56||built computer, display has a green tint||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||03-29-2001||11:44 PM||||I ran around today, bought everything I needed, and assembled a new computer this afternoon.<P>At first power-up the dispay had a green tint to it. This tint lasted through the HD installation, the Windows installation, and even the motherboard chipset software installation. I can't get rid of it. The monitor I am using is one we have been using daily, so the monitor is good.<BR>The board is an Intel D815EEA with a Celeron processor(PPGA 370). I think it has something to do with the video drivers not configured properly, but can't find any problem. <P>Any thoughts on what to check or what I missed. Everything works perfect other than looking at the display is like looking at a monitor through green glasses.||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||03-29-2001||11:50 PM||||check connections, make sure everything is tight.  Monitor may have a self-test.  Unplug it from the pc while it's powered-on and a message may appear.  If the self-test shows all colors correctly, the monitor is probably fine(unless it's the cable.)  If the green tint exists even before windows is entered(ie. memory check, etc...) then you've probably got a video card issue.  you can try reseating the video card in a different slot(if it's not an agp card) while the comp is off, but more than likely it's the video card itself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||03-30-2001||12:46 AM||||It's not getting one of the color signals.  If you've tried the monitor before and know it's good, then you've either got a bent pin on the video cable, a bad video cable, a loose or bad video card.  That's about it.  90% of the time, it's a loose cable or bent pin where the monitor plugs into the video card.  This happens a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-30-2001||01:15 AM||||WOW!!! This thing is fast! My first windows installation was really bad, lots of errors, bad CD. This is second windows installation with bad cd but good copy onto computer. 700Mhz with 288Mb RAM doesn't even compare to my 200Mhz notebook with 16Mb RAM. I need to make one of these for me.<P>I just checked the cable, and I have a bent pin(actually pushed into the rubber block, don't think its fixable. The cable isn't removable from the monitor. Is it possible to replace the end? or???||||reg||11
Z||000004||fiero56||03-30-2001||02:43 AM||||I searched everywhere for my "micro" needle-nose pliars, but they were no-where to be found. So, I grabbed an old pair of pliars, and ground them to a fine point on the end. Took a little but of wrestling with the pins, but I got the bent one back out and and in its proper place. The display is now the proper output. I'm going to file this in "learned experiences" so I'll know what it is the next time it happens. ||||reg||1
